1790607796.332	
1790607796.332	In China, block printing (xylography) developed rapidly during the Tang Dynasty (618–907 CE). Xylography involves carving an entire page—each character or image—into a single wooden block. This process is labor-intensive compared to movable type, as an entire page must be carved anew for each edition, but it proved that complex visual and textual information could be reliably reproduced beyond manual transcription. Early uses were primarily religious in nature, driven by Buddhist monasteries needing to disseminate scriptures across vast geographical regions.
